# Object-oriented Forth (objects.fs)

Gforth-only. Load `objects.fs` when the project already uses this model — do not introduce OOP for problems solvable with `create`/`does>` or structs.

## Class and selector

```forth
require objects.fs

object class
  selector draw ( x y obj -- )
end-class graphical

graphical class
  cell% field circle-radius
:noname ( x y circle -- )
  circle-radius @ draw-circle ;
overrides draw
:noname ( n circle -- )
  circle-radius ! ;
overrides construct
end-class circle
```

`selector` declares an operation; child classes **`overrides`** methods. Parent without overrides is abstract (no concrete `draw`).

## Instances

```forth
50 circle heap-new constant my-circle
100 100 my-circle draw
```

Use **`heap-new`** / **`destroy`** for heap objects; dictionary instances use `new` patterns from the project's objects.fs usage.

## When not to use

Prefer plain Forth data + words for challenges and small utilities. See `forth-defining.mdc` for `defer`/`is`, `forth-dialect-gforth.mdc` for `begin-structure`. OOF/mini-oof are alternate models — match the project, do not mix casually.
